[[Presidential election]]s were held in [[Colombia]] on 29 May 2022, with a [[Two-round system|runoff]] on 19 June 2022 as no candidate obtained at least 50% in the first round of voting. [[Iván Duque]], who was elected president in [[2018 Colombian presidential election|2018]], was ineligible to run due to term limits.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Tejada |first1=Carlos |last2=Turkewitz |first2=Julie |date=2021-08-30 |title=Colombia's Troubles Put a President's Legacy on the Line |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/30/world/americas/colombia-president-duque.html |access-date=2022-06-23 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=23 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220623012232/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/08/30/world/americas/colombia-president-duque.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Gustavo Petro]], a [[Senate of Colombia|senator]], former [[Mayor of Bogotá]], and runner-up in the [[2018 Colombian presidential election|2018 election]], defeated [[Rodolfo Hernández Suárez]], former mayor of [[Bucaramanga]], in the runoff election.<ref name=":0">{{cite news |title=Colombia's presidential race heads to runoff |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/29/colombias-presidential-race-heads-to-runoff |access-date=29 May 2022 |work=Al Jazeera |date=29 May 2022 |archive-date=29 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220529231802/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/5/29/colombias-presidential-race-heads-to-runoff |url-status=live }}</ref> Petro's victory was considered the first [[left-wing]] candidate to be elected [[president of Colombia]] in the Colombian recent history,<ref name=":1">{{cite news |date=19 June 2022 |title=Leftist Gustavo Petro wins Colombian presidency |work=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/33d30a93-0f48-41ee-9847-4503c79ca7d9 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |access-date=19 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220620074307/https://www.ft.com/content/33d30a93-0f48-41ee-9847-4503c79ca7d9 |archive-date=20 June 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Bocanegra |first1=Nelson |last2=Griffin |first2=Oliver |last3=Vargas |first3=Carlos |date=19 June 2022 |title=Colombia elects former guerrilla Petro as first leftist president|publisher=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombians-head-polls-tightest-election-recent-memory-2022-06-19/ |url-status=live |access-date=19 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220620074307/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombians-head-polls-tightest-election-recent-memory-2022-06-19/ |archive-date=20 June 2022}}</ref> and his running mate, [[Francia Márquez]], is the first [[Afro-Colombian]] elected to the [[Vice President of Colombia|vice-presidency]],<ref name=":01">{{cite news |last1=Turkewitz |first1=Julie |last2=Glatsky |first2=Genevieve |date=2022-06-21 |title=He Promised to Transform Colombia as President. Can He Fulfill That Vow? |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/20/world/americas/colombia-gustavo-petro-president.html |access-date=2022-06-23 |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=23 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220623012235/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/20/world/americas/colombia-gustavo-petro-president.html |url-status=live }}</ref> as well as the second female vice-president overall.<ref name="The Dialogue 2022">{{cite web|last1=Carlsen|first1=Laura|last2=Dickinson|first2=Elizabeth|last3=Dimitroff|first3=Sashe|last4=Guzmán|first4=Sergio|last5=Molina|first5=Marco|last6=Shifter|first6=Michael|last7=Velez de Berliner|first7=Maria|date=21 June 2022|url=https://www.thedialogue.org/analysis/what-will-petros-presidency-mean-for-colombia/|title=What Will Petro's Presidency Mean for Colombia?|website=The Dialogue|publisher=Inter-American Dialogue|access-date=25 June 2022|archive-date=21 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220621183820/https://www.thedialogue.org/analysis/what-will-petros-presidency-mean-for-colombia/|url-status=live}}</ref>

The elections were held in the aftermath of the [[2021 Colombian protests]] amid poor economic conditions during the country's [[COVID-19 pandemic in Colombia|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/> Petro, a former [[AD/M-19]] member who was defeated by Duque by over ten percentage points in 2018,<ref name=":2" /> was chosen as a candidate of the [[Historic Pact for Colombia]] alliance. Petro's left-wing platform encompassed support for [[land reform]], [[universal health care]], continuing the [[Colombian peace process]], and expanding [[social services]].<ref name=":01"/><ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/>

Hernández, an independent affiliated with the [[League of Anti-Corruption Governors]], ran a [[populist]] campaign that emphasized support for [[Law and order (politics)|law and order]] policies and anti-corruption efforts.<ref name=":3"/><ref name="Dyer 2022">{{cite web|last=Dyer|first=Gwynne|date=15 June 2022|url=https://www.theportugalnews.com/news/2022-06-15/latin-america-the-pink-tide-is-rising/67888|title=Latin America: The Pink Tide Is Rising|website=The Portugal News|access-date=23 June 2022|archive-date=17 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220617110915/https://www.theportugalnews.com/news/2022-06-15/latin-america-the-pink-tide-is-rising/67888|url-status=live}}</ref> Hernández experienced a surge in support in the final weeks of the campaign, which allowed him to overtake conservative candidate [[Federico Gutiérrez]] for a spot in the runoff. This surge in popularity was partially credited to his substantial social media following and [[TikTok]] videos,<ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/> which led him to be dubbed the "King of TikTok".<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":4" /><ref name=":5" />

Petro won the runoff with 50.42% of the vote to Hernández's 47.35%.<ref name=esr>{{cite web |title=En la segunda vuelta presidencial del 2022 se registró la abstención más baja de los últimos 24 años |url=https://www.registraduria.gov.co/En-la-segunda-vuelta-presidencial-del-2022-se-registro-la-abstencion-mas-baja.html |publisher=National Register of Colombia |access-date=14 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706123801/https://www.registraduria.gov.co/En-la-segunda-vuelta-presidencial-del-2022-se-registro-la-abstencion-mas-baja.html |archive-date=6 July 2022 |language=es |date=21 June 2022 |url-status=live}}</ref> Petro dominated in [[Bogotá]] and regions on Colombia's [[Caribbean]] and [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific]] coasts,<ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/><ref name="Marsh 2022">{{cite web|last=Marsh|first=Aygen|date=21 June 2022|url=https://amicohoops.net/where-did-the-2-7-million-new-votes-for-gustavo-petro-come-from-colombias-presidential-election/|title=Where Did The 2.7 Million New Votes For Gustavo Petro Come From Colombia's Presidential Election|website=Amico Hoops|access-date=24 June 2022|archive-date=23 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220623181038/https://amicohoops.net/where-did-the-2-7-million-new-votes-for-gustavo-petro-come-from-colombias-presidential-election/|url-status=live}}</ref> and received over 81% of the vote in the coastal [[Departments of Colombia|department]] of [[Chocó Department|Chocó]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Guzmán|first=Sergio|date=21 June 2022|title=Key Takeaways from Colombia's Presidential Elections|url=https://theglobalamericans.org/2022/06/key-takeaways-from-colombias-presidential-elections/|access-date=23 June 2022|publisher=Global Americans|archive-date=21 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220621181946/https://theglobalamericans.org/2022/06/key-takeaways-from-colombias-presidential-elections/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Due to an increased turnout among his supporters, Petro received nearly 2.7 million more votes in the second round than the first.<ref name="Marsh 2022"/><ref name="Galindo 2022">{{cite news|last=Galindo|first=Jorge|date=20 June 2022|url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2022-06-20/how-colombia-shifted-to-the-left.html|title=How Colombia shifted to the left|newspaper=El País|access-date=24 June 2022|archive-date=24 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220624000845/https://english.elpais.com/international/2022-06-20/how-colombia-shifted-to-the-left.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The result was noted for a continuing trend of left-wing victories in Latin America,<ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/> which has been dubbed as a "new [[pink tide]]".<ref name="Dyer 2022"/><ref>{{cite news|last1=Araujo|first1=Gabriel|last2=Vargas|first2=Carlos|last3=Woodford|first3=Isabel|date=22 June 2022|url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/latin-americas-new-pink-tide-gains-pace-colombia-shifts-left-brazil-up-next-2022-06-22/|title=Latin America's new 'pink tide' gains pace as Colombia shifts left; Brazil up next|publisher=Reuters|access-date=23 June 2022|archive-date=28 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220628195416/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/latin-americas-new-pink-tide-gains-pace-colombia-shifts-left-brazil-up-next-2022-06-22/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Garavito|first1=Tatiana|last2=Thanki|first2=Nathan|date=23 June 2022|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2022/6/23/colombias-shift-to-the-left-a-new-pink-tide-in-latin-america|title=Colombia's shift to the left: A new 'pink tide' in Latin America?|publisher=Al Jazeera|access-date=24 June 2022|archive-date=23 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220623211720/https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2022/6/23/colombias-shift-to-the-left-a-new-pink-tide-in-latin-america|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Background==
{{further|2018 Colombian presidential election|2021 Colombian protests}}
During the previous election held in 2018, a run-off took place as no candidate attained 50% of the vote. The top two candidates were senator [[Iván Duque]] of the [[Democratic Center (Colombia)|Democratic Center]] party and [[Humane Colombia]] nominee [[Gustavo Petro]], a former mayor of Bogotá and a former [[AD/M-19]] member.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Joe Parkin Daniels |author2=Ed Vulliamy |title=Colombia elections: rightwinger and former guerrilla head for presidential runoff |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/may/28/colombia-presidential-elections-ivan-duque-gustavo-petro-runoff |access-date=8 May 2022 |work=The Guardian|location=Bogotá; Medellín |date=27 May 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220331190730/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/may/28/colombia-presidential-elections-ivan-duque-gustavo-petro-runoff |archive-date=31 March 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> The election's issues included the [[FARC peace agreement]], [[Corruption in Colombia|corruption]], unemployment, and [[Health care in Colombia|healthcare]].<ref>{{cite web |author1=Holly K. Sonneland |title=Explainer: Colombia's 2018 Elections |url=https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-colombias-2018-elections |publisher=AS/COA |access-date=8 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220415173141/https://www.as-coa.org/articles/explainer-colombias-2018-elections |archive-date=15 April 2022 |date=28 June 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> Duque defeated Petro by over ten percentage points;<ref name=":2">{{cite news |author1=Joe Parkin Daniels |title=Iván Duque wins election to become Colombia's president |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/jun/18/ivan-duque-wins-election-to-become-colombias-president |access-date=8 May 2022 |work=The Guardian |date=18 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220505030820/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2018/jun/18/ivan-duque-wins-election-to-become-colombias-president |archive-date=5 May 2022 |url-status=live|location=Bogotá}}</ref> however, there were subsequent allegations of fraud and irregularities.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Adriaan Alsema |title=Colombia's electoral authority investigating alleged 2018 election fraud |url=https://colombiareports.com/colombias-electoral-authority-investigating-alleged-2018-election-fraud/ |access-date=8 May 2022 |work=Colombia Reports |date=11 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220407045658/https://colombiareports.com/colombias-electoral-authority-investigating-alleged-2018-election-fraud/ |archive-date=7 April 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> As the runner up, Petro became a senator per the Legislative Act No. 2 of 2015.<ref>{{cite news |title=Meet Gustavo Petro, Colombian Former Guerrilla & Leftist Who Mounted Historic Campaign for Presidency |url=https://www.democracynow.org/2018/8/10/meet_gustavo_petro_colombian_former_guerilla |access-date=8 May 2022 |work=Democracy Now |date=10 August 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422060718/https://www.democracynow.org/2018/8/10/meet_gustavo_petro_colombian_former_guerilla |archive-date=22 April 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ActoLegislativo02/2015">{{cite news |author=Secretaria General del Senado |title=Acto Legislativo número 02 de 2015 |url=http://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/acto_legislativo_02_2015.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220129055536/http://www.secretariasenado.gov.co/senado/basedoc/acto_legislativo_02_2015.html |archive-date=29 January 2022 |access-date=28 April 2021 |website=Secretariasenado.gov.co |language=es |quote=El candidato que le siga en votos a quien la autoridad electoral declare elegido en el cargo de Presidente y Vicepresidente de la República, Gobernador de Departamento, Alcalde Distrital y Alcalde municipal tendrá el derecho personal a ocupar una curul en el Senado, Cámara de Representantes, Asamblea Departamental, Concejo Distrital y Concejo Municipal, respectivamente, durante el período de la correspondiente corporación.}}</ref>

Widespread demonstrations against the policies of president Duque took place from late April to December 2021. Amid the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Colombia]], which had dealt a blow to the economy and at a time when unemployment rates were high, Duque proposed a tax increase.<ref>{{cite news |author1=John Otis |title=Protesters eMarch In Colombia Against Plan To Raise Taxes In Pandemic-Wracked Economy Facebook Twitter Flipboard Email |url=https://www.npr.org/2021/04/30/992142575/protesters-march-in-colombia-against-plan-to-raise-taxes-in-pandemic-wracked-eco |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=NPR |date=30 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210501210242/https://www.npr.org/2021/04/30/992142575/protesters-march-in-colombia-against-plan-to-raise-taxes-in-pandemic-wracked-eco |archive-date=1 May 2021 |url-status=live|location=Bogotá}}</ref> Furthermore, a controversial bill was proposed in Congress that would have resulted in the [[privatization]] of healthcare.<ref>{{cite news |title=Armando Benedetti anuncia que pedirá el retiro de la reforma a la salud |url=https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2021/05/02/reforma-a-la-salud-el-siguiente-punto-en-disputa-por-el-que-las-protestas-en-colombia-se-mantendran/ |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=Infobae |date=2 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328192407/https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2021/05/02/reforma-a-la-salud-el-siguiente-punto-en-disputa-por-el-que-las-protestas-en-colombia-se-mantendran/ |archive-date=28 March 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> The majority of the protests were peaceful, with some cases of vandalism.<ref>{{cite news |title=Paro nacional en Cali: el MIO afectado por ataques de manifestantes |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/colombia/cali/paro-nacional-cali-mio-afectado-por-ataques-de-manifestantes-584396 |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=El Tiempo |date=28 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328192937/https://www.eltiempo.com/colombia/cali/paro-nacional-cali-mio-afectado-por-ataques-de-manifestantes-584396 |archive-date=28 March 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> According to human-rights groups, police reacted violently to protesters in various instances, leading to deaths and alleged cases of sexual assault.<ref>{{cite news |title=Colombia: al menos 19 muertos y más de 800 heridos en las protestas contra la reforma fiscal |url=https://www.bbc.com/mundo/noticias-america-latina-56973896 |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=BBC Mundo |date=3 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406073842/https://www.bbc.com/mundo/noticias-america-latina-56973896 |archive-date=6 April 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=José Miguel Vivanco asegura que en Colombia la fuerza pública tiene "problemas estructurales de militarización" |url=https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2021/05/13/jose-miguel-vivanco-asegura-que-en-colombia-la-fuerza-publica-tiene-problemas-estructurales-de-militarizacion/ |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=Infobae |date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328192931/https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2021/05/13/jose-miguel-vivanco-asegura-que-en-colombia-la-fuerza-publica-tiene-problemas-estructurales-de-militarizacion/ |archive-date=28 March 2022 |language=es}}</ref> The protests led to a withdrawal of the healthcare and tax reform bills and the resignation of finance minister [[Alberto Carrasquilla Barrera]].<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/19/world/americas/colombia-protests-police.html |title=From Colombia to U.S., Police Violence Pushes Protests Into Mass Movements |date=19 May 2021 |last=Taub |first=Amanda |work=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=10 May 2022 |archive-date=22 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210522052050/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/19/world/americas/colombia-protests-police.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Es oficial: Iván Duque acepta renuncia de Alberto Carrasquilla como minhacienda |url=https://www.elespectador.com/economia/es-oficial-ivan-duque-acepta-renuncia-de-alberto-carrasquilla-como-minhacienda-article/ |access-date=10 May 2022 |work=El Espectador |date=4 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006202400/https://www.elespectador.com/economia/es-oficial-ivan-duque-acepta-renuncia-de-alberto-carrasquilla-como-minhacienda-article/ |archive-date=6 October 2021 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
{{further|Elections in Colombia|Politics of Colombia}}{{Excerpt|Elections in Colombia#Electoral system|Presidential|subsections=yes|hat=no}}The National Registry confirmed that the minimum number of signatures required was 580,620, equivalent to 3% of the total valid vote in the [[2018 Colombian presidential election]], and that they had to be delivered to the registry by 17 December 2021.<ref>{{cite news |title=Registraduría aclara reglas de juego para precandidatos por firmas |url=https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/requisitos-que-fijo-la-registraduria-para-ser-candidato-presidencial-por-firmas-JJ15852496 |newspaper=[[El Colombiano]] |language=es |date=8 October 2021 |access-date=2 March 2022 |archive-date=2 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220302145035/https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/requisitos-que-fijo-la-registraduria-para-ser-candidato-presidencial-por-firmas-JJ15852496 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 17 December, the National Registry confirmed that seven pre-candidates had delivered the necessary number of signatures: [[Alejandro Char]], [[Rodolfo Hernández Suárez|Rodolfo Hernández]], [[Federico Gutiérrez]], [[Alejandro Gaviria]], Luis Pérez, [[Roy Barreras]], and [[Juan Carlos Echeverry (politician)|Juan Carlos Echeverry]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Siete aspirantes a la Presidencia inscribieron sus candidaturas por firmas |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/siete-aspirantes-a-la-presidencia-inscribieron-sus-candidaturas-por-firmas/ |newspaper=[[El Espectador]] |language=es |date=17 December 2021 |access-date=2 March 2022 |archive-date=2 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220302145848/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/siete-aspirantes-a-la-presidencia-inscribieron-sus-candidaturas-por-firmas/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Of these seven pre-candidates, Char, Gutiérrez, and Gaviria accepted the endorsements of political parties, thereby bypassing the necessity to run as independents, while Barreras and Echeverry later decided to drop out of the presidential race; this left Hernández and Pérez as the only independent candidates in the race.<ref>{{cite news |title=Roy Barreras declina su candidatura a la presidencia |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/politica/partidos-politicos/roy-barreras-declina-su-candidatura-a-la-presidencia-637787 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=9 December 2021 |access-date=2 March 2022 |archive-date=3 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220303000941/https://www.eltiempo.com/politica/partidos-politicos/roy-barreras-declina-su-candidatura-a-la-presidencia-637787 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=El exministro Juan Carlos Echeverry desistió de su candidatura presidencial |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/el-exministro-juan-carlos-echeverry-desistio-de-su-candidatura-presidencial/ |newspaper=El Espectador |language=es |date=13 January 2022 |access-date=2 March 2022 |archive-date=3 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220303011517/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/el-exministro-juan-carlos-echeverry-desistio-de-su-candidatura-presidencial/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Primaries and party conventions==
{{Further|2022 Colombian presidential primaries}}

===Historic Pact for Colombia===
The [[Historic Pact for Colombia]] (Spanish: ''Pacto Histórico por Colombia'') is a coalition of left-wing, progressive, and Indigenous politicians. Five pre-candidates representing six political parties or movements announced that they would be standing for election as the unified presidential candidate for the coalition. The candidate was chosen by public vote on 13 March 2022.<ref>{{cite news |title=Petro será el primero del tarjetón de la consulta del Pacto Histórico |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/petro-sera-el-primero-del-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-del-pacto-historico/ |newspaper=[[El Espectador]] |language=es |date=4 February 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=17 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220317065606/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/petro-sera-el-primero-del-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-del-pacto-historico/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Gustavo Petro was announced as the winner of the public vote and was nominated to be the candidate of the Historic Pact for Colombia coalition.<ref>{{cite news |last=Neira Goyeneche |first=Armando |title=Gustavo Petro gana consulta del Pacto Histórico y estas son sus propuestas |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/gustavo-petro-quien-es-y-cuales-son-su-propuestas-pacto-historico-656335 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=13 March 2022 |access-date=13 March 2022 |archive-date=16 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220316053511/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/gustavo-petro-quien-es-y-cuales-son-su-propuestas-pacto-historico-656335 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Hope Center Coalition===
The [[Hope Center Coalition]] (Spanish: ''Coalición Centro Esperanza''), formerly known as the Coalition of Hope (Spanish: ''Coalición de la Esperanza'') until 28 November 2021,<ref>{{cite news |title=Humo blanco: nace Coalición Centro Esperanza para las elecciones presidenciales |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/humo-blanco-nace-coalicion-centro-esperanza-para-las-elecciones-presidenciales/ |newspaper=El Espectador |language=es |date=28 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301210753/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/humo-blanco-nace-coalicion-centro-esperanza-para-las-elecciones-presidenciales/ |url-status=live }}</ref> is a coalition of centre and centre-left politicians. Five pre-candidates announced that they would be standing for election as the unified presidential candidate for the coalition. The candidate was chosen by public vote on 13 March 2022.<ref>{{cite news |title=Así quedó el orden del tarjetón de la consulta de la Coalición Centro Esperanza |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/asi-quedo-el-orden-del-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-de-la-coalicion-centro-esperanza/ |newspaper=El Espectador |language=es |date=2 February 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301175044/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/asi-quedo-el-orden-del-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-de-la-coalicion-centro-esperanza/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Sergio Fajardo]] was announced as the winner of the public vote and was nominated to be the candidate of the Hope Center Coalition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Sergio Fajardo gana la consulta Centro Esperanza y estas son sus propuestas |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/sergio-fajardo-quien-es-y-sus-propuestas-presidenciales-para-2022-656363 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=13 March 2022 |access-date=13 March 2022 |archive-date=14 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314063809/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/sergio-fajardo-quien-es-y-sus-propuestas-presidenciales-para-2022-656363 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Team for Colombia Coalition===
The [[Team for Colombia]] Coalition (Spanish: ''Coalición Equipo por Colombia'') is a coalition of centre-right and right-wing politicians. Five pre-candidates announced that they would be standing for election as the unified presidential candidate for the coalition. The candidate was chosen by public vote on 13 March 2022.<ref>{{cite news |title=Así será el tarjetón de la consulta del Equipo por Colombia |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/asi-sera-el-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-del-equipo-por-colombia/ |newspaper=El Espectador |language=es |date=4 February 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=17 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220317065922/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/elecciones-colombia-2022/asi-sera-el-tarjeton-de-la-consulta-del-equipo-por-colombia/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Federico Gutiérrez]] was announced as the winner of the public vote and was nominated to be the candidate of the Team for Colombia Coalition.<ref>{{cite news |last=Mercado |first=Alejandro |title=Fico Gutiérrez gana consulta Equipo por Colombia y estas son sus propuestas |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/fico-gutierrez-quien-es-y-sus-propuestas-para-la-presidencia-en-2022-656487 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=13 March 2022 |access-date=13 March 2022 |archive-date=14 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314152753/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/fico-gutierrez-quien-es-y-sus-propuestas-para-la-presidencia-en-2022-656487 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Other candidates===
* [[Rodolfo Hernández Suárez|Rodolfo Hernández]], former mayor of Bucaramanga (League of Anti-Corruption Governors). Hernández announced on 29 June 2021 that he would run as an independent candidate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Rodolfo Hernández inscribio su candidatura para la Presidencia en 2022 |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/colombia/santander/rodolfo-hernandez-inscribio-su-candidatura-para-la-presidencia-en-2022-599687 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=29 June 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301184200/https://www.eltiempo.com/colombia/santander/rodolfo-hernandez-inscribio-su-candidatura-para-la-presidencia-en-2022-599687 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 13 December 2021, Hernández announced that he had delivered nearly 1.9 million signatures to the National Registry in support of his candidacy.<ref>{{cite news |last=Cortés |first=Sebastián |title=Con pulla a Petro, Rodolfo Hernández entregó las firmas para su candidatura |url=https://caracol.com.co/radio/2021/12/13/politica/1639421910_551321.html |website=[[Caracol Radio]] |language=es |date=13 December 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301185424/https://caracol.com.co/radio/2021/12/13/politica/1639421910_551321.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[John Milton Rodríguez]], senator (Fair and Free Colombia). Rodríguez was chosen as the candidate of the evangelical Christian party Fair and Free Colombia (Spanish: ''Colombia Justa Libres'') at the party's national convention in November 2021, obtaining 75% of the delegates' votes.<ref>{{cite web |last=Correa Escobar |first=María Kamila |title=John Milton Rodríguez es el candidato presidencial único de Colombia Justa Libres |url=https://www.wradio.com.co/noticias/actualidad/john-milton-rodriguez-es-el-candidato-presidencial-unico-de-colombia-justa-libres/20211114/nota/4178264.aspx |website=[[W Radio (Colombia)|W Radio]] |language=es |date=14 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301185955/https://www.wradio.com.co/noticias/actualidad/john-milton-rodriguez-es-el-candidato-presidencial-unico-de-colombia-justa-libres/20211114/nota/4178264.aspx |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[Enrique Gómez Martínez]] (National Salvation Movement). On 1 November 2021, Gómez Martínez announced that he was relaunching the conservative National Salvation Movement (Spanish: ''Movimiento Salvación Nacional'') that had been founded by his late uncle [[Álvaro Gómez Hurtado]].<ref>{{cite news |last=González Penagos |first=Javier |title="Hoy termina la gesta judicial y nace la política": Enrique Gómez Martínez |url=https://www.elespectador.com/politica/hoy-termina-la-gesta-judicial-y-nace-la-politica-enrique-gomez-martinez-sobre-salvacion-nacional/ |newspaper=El Espectador |language=es |date=1 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=7 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211207080222/https://www.elespectador.com/politica/hoy-termina-la-gesta-judicial-y-nace-la-politica-enrique-gomez-martinez-sobre-salvacion-nacional/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On 1 December 2021, the National Registry accepted the return of the National Salvation Movement as a political party, and Gómez Martínez became the party's presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web |last=Ramírez |first=Fabián |title=Enrique Gómez Martínez será candidato presidencial del Partido Salvación Nacional |url=https://noticias.caracoltv.com/codigo-caracol/enrique-gomez-martinez-sera-candidato-presidencial-del-partido-salvacion-nacional |website=[[Caracol TV]] |language=es |date=1 December 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301191534/https://noticias.caracoltv.com/codigo-caracol/enrique-gomez-martinez-sera-candidato-presidencial-del-partido-salvacion-nacional |url-status=live }}</ref>

