===Withdrew===
*[[Óscar Iván Zuluaga]], former [[Ministry of Finance and Public Credit|Minister of Finance and Public Credit]] and runner-up in the [[2014 Colombian presidential election]] (Democratic Center). It was announced that the candidate for the right wing Democratic Center party would be chosen via two polls conducted internally during November 2021.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hoy empiezan las encuestas del uribismo para elegir a su candidato presidencial |url=https://www.rcnradio.com/politica/hoy-empiezan-las-encuestas-del-uribismo-para-elegir-su-candidato-presidencial |website=[[RCN Radio]] |language=es |date=10 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301174054/https://www.rcnradio.com/politica/hoy-empiezan-las-encuestas-del-uribismo-para-elegir-su-candidato-presidencial |url-status=live }}</ref> The polls were conducted by the two polling agencies Centro Nacional de Consultoría (CNC) and YanHass via telephone, and polled 2,100 party members (comprising 25% of the final weighting) and 2,100 members of the public (comprising the remaining 75% weighting).<ref name="ElPais20211127">{{cite news |title=Centro Democrático publicó los resultados de sus encuestas para escoger candidato único |url=https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/centro-democratico-publico-los-resultados-de-sus-encuestas-para-escoger-candidato-unico.html |newspaper=El País |language=es |date=27 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301174056/https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/centro-democratico-publico-los-resultados-de-sus-encuestas-para-escoger-candidato-unico.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ElTiempo20211122">{{cite news |title=Óscar Iván Zuluaga repite: será candidato presidencial del CD para el 2022 |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/politica/partidos-politicos/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-el-nuevo-candidato-del-centro-democratico-633908 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=22 November 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=4 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220404224707/https://www.eltiempo.com/politica/partidos-politicos/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-el-nuevo-candidato-del-centro-democratico-633908 |url-status=live }}</ref> In both polls Zuluaga emerged comfortably as the preferred candidate, winning 53% of the vote in the CNC poll and 52% of the vote in the YanHass poll, and he was announced as the Democratic Center's candidate on 22 November 2021.<ref name="ElTiempo20211122" /> He beat four other pre-candidates: [[María Fernanda Cabal]] (23% and 27% in the CNC and YanHass polls, respectively), [[Paloma Valencia]] (11% and 9%), [[Alirio Barrera]] (8% and 7%), and Rafael Nieto Loiaza (5% and 5%).<ref name="ElPais20211127" /> The result of the vote caused controversy, with Cabal in particular disputing the results of the polls and accusing current president [[Iván Duque]] (also of the Democratic Center party) of interfering with the voting process to ensure that Zuluaga would win.<ref>{{cite news |title=María Fernanda Cabal: 'Duque le apostó a Zuluaga para sacarme del juego' |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/maria-fernanda-cabal-dice-que-ivan-duque-le-aposto-a-oscar-ivan-zuluaga-655007 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=1 March 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301140436/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/maria-fernanda-cabal-dice-que-ivan-duque-le-aposto-a-oscar-ivan-zuluaga-655007 |url-status=live }}</ref> Following his victory, Zuluaga was invited to join the Team for Colombia Coalition, but he originally declined the invitation.<ref>{{cite news |title=Óscar Iván Zuluaga reafirma que no se unirá a la coalición Equipo por Colombia |url=https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-reafirma-que-no-se-unira-a-la-coalicion-equipo-por-colombia.html |newspaper=El País |language=es |date=20 January 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301174054/https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-reafirma-que-no-se-unira-a-la-coalicion-equipo-por-colombia.html |url-status=live }}</ref> However, following Federico Gutiérrez's nomination as the Team for Colombia candidate, Zuluaga withdrew his candidacy and stated that he would support Gutiérrez's campaign.<ref>{{cite news |title=Óscar Iván Zuluaga renuncia a su candidatura presidencial |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-renuncia-a-su-candidatura-presidencial-658296 |newspaper=El Tiempo |language=es |date=14 March 2022 |access-date=16 March 2022 |archive-date=16 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220316044901/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/oscar-ivan-zuluaga-renuncia-a-su-candidatura-presidencial-658296 |url-status=live }}</ref>
* [[Luis Gilberto Murillo]], former governor of [[Chocó Department|Chocó]] and former [[Ministry of Environment and Sustainable Development (Colombia)|Minister of Environment and Sustainable Development]] (Colombia Reborn). Murillo had been expected to be a pre-candidate for the Hope Center Coalition, but on 21 January 2022 he announced that he would run independently as the candidate for the Colombia Reborn (Spanish: ''Colombia Renaciente'') party.<ref>{{cite news |title=¿Ruptura? Luis Gilberto Murillo no hará parte de la Coalición Centro Esperanza |url=https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/luis-gilberto-murillo-sera-candidato-presidencial-fuera-de-la-coalicion-de-la-esperanza-KD16404385 |magazine=El Colombiano |language=es |date=21 January 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301214106/https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/luis-gilberto-murillo-sera-candidato-presidencial-fuera-de-la-coalicion-de-la-esperanza-KD16404385 |url-status=live }}</ref> He later stated that he had not felt welcome within the Hope Center Coalition.<ref>{{cite magazine |title="Sentí que no era bienvenido en la coalición": Luis Gilberto Murillo |url=https://www.semana.com/nacion/articulo/senti-que-no-era-bienvenido-en-la-coalicion-luis-gilberto-murillo/202211/ |magazine=Semana |language=es |date=11 February 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=3 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220303205922/https://www.semana.com/nacion/articulo/senti-que-no-era-bienvenido-en-la-coalicion-luis-gilberto-murillo/202211/ |url-status=live }}</ref> However, on 17 March he announced he would suspend his presidential bid to become the vice presidential nominee of the Hope Center Coalition.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/luis-gilberto-murillo-formula-de-fajardo-habla-de-los-proyectos-659137|title=Luis Murillo, candidato a Vicepresidencia: 'Llegar a la Colombia profunda'|date=17 March 2022|work=El Tiempo|access-date=4 April 2022|language=es|archive-date=6 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220406233913/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/candidatos/luis-gilberto-murillo-formula-de-fajardo-habla-de-los-proyectos-659137|url-status=live}}</ref>
*Luis Pérez, former mayor of Medellín and former governor of [[Antioquia Department|Antioquia]] (Colombia Think Big). Pérez announced on 26 February 2021 that he would be running for the presidency as an independent candidate.<ref>{{cite news |title=Exgobernador de Antioquia anuncia que será candidato presidencial |url=https://www.eluniversal.com.co/colombia/exgobernador-de-antioquia-anuncia-que-sera-candidato-presidencial-BH4238030 |newspaper=El Universal |language=es |date=26 February 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301194621/https://www.eluniversal.com.co/colombia/exgobernador-de-antioquia-anuncia-que-sera-candidato-presidencial-BH4238030 |url-status=live }}</ref> On 15 December 2021, it was confirmed that Pérez had received the necessary number of signatures to be officially declared as a candidate.<ref>{{cite magazine |title=Con un millón de firmas, Luis Pérez oficializó su candidatura presidencial |url=https://www.semana.com/confidenciales/articulo/con-un-millon-de-firmas-luis-perez-oficializo-su-candidatura-presidencial/202118/ |magazine=Semana |language=es |date=15 December 2021 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=11 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220311214050/https://www.semana.com/confidenciales/articulo/con-un-millon-de-firmas-luis-perez-oficializo-su-candidatura-presidencial/202118/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On 11 May 2022, Peréz withdrew his candidacy, citing a "hateful" and "polarised" political atmosphere. He stated that in the presidential debates, there is "no room for ideas, [but] only for insults and fights." Pérez also said that he intends to "return love to politics, so people do not vote out of fear".<ref>{{cite news |title=El exgobernador Luis Pérez renuncia a su candidatura presidencial |url=https://www.colombia.com/actualidad/politica/el-exgobernador-luis-perez-renuncia-a-su-candidatura-presidencial-350520 |website=Colombia.com|access-date=12 May 2022 |date=11 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220511152115/https://www.colombia.com/actualidad/politica/el-exgobernador-luis-perez-renuncia-a-su-candidatura-presidencial-350520 |archive-date=11 May 2022 |language=es}}</ref>
* [[Íngrid Betancourt]], former senator and member of the [[Oxygen Green Party]]. Betancourt announced her candidacy on 18 January 2022<ref>{{cite web |title=Ingrid Betancourt: Former Farc captive announces presidential bid |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-60048394 |website=BBC |date=19 January 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=1 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220301181128/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-60048394 |url-status=live }}</ref> and originally joined the Hope Center Coalition. However, following a dispute with fellow coalition pre-candidate Alejandro Gaviria, Betancourt declared on 29 January that she was leaving the coalition and would run as an independent candidate under her own Oxygen Green (Spanish: ''Verde Oxígeno'') party.<ref>{{cite news |last=Torrado |first=Santiago |title=Ingrid Betancourt abandona la coalición de centro en Colombia |url=https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-01-29/ingrid-betancourt-abandona-la-coalicion-de-centro-en-colombia.html |newspaper=El País |language=es |date=29 January 2022 |access-date=1 March 2022 |archive-date=14 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314185903/https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-01-29/ingrid-betancourt-abandona-la-coalicion-de-centro-en-colombia.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Following a poor showing in the polls, Betancourt withdrew from the race on 20 May 2022 and endorsed Hernández Suárez.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ingrid Betancourt renuncia a la candidatura: adhiere a Rodolfo Hernández |url=https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/ingrid-betancourt-se-une-a-la-campana-de-rodolfo-hernandez-673982 |access-date=21 May 2022 |work=El Tiempo |date=20 May 2022 |language=es |archive-date=21 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220521003751/https://www.eltiempo.com/elecciones-2022/presidencia/ingrid-betancourt-se-une-a-la-campana-de-rodolfo-hernandez-673982 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Campaign==
The economist, former guerrilla, and former mayor of Bogotá, [[Gustavo Petro]], previously a candidate in the 2010 and 2018 Colombian presidential elections, maintained a lead in most opinion polls and was set during the election to become the first president of Colombia from a [[left-wing]] coalition.<ref name="auto">{{Cite news|url=https://americasquarterly.org/article/meet-the-candidates-colombia/|title=Meet the Candidates: Colombia|work=Americas Quarterly|date=4 November 2021|access-date=4 April 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220512073940/https://americasquarterly.org/article/meet-the-candidates-colombia/|archive-date=12 May 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> In September 2021, Petro promised that he would retire from politics should his campaign for the presidency be unsuccessful. Petro said he would do so as he did not want to be an "eternal candidate".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Martínez Ahrens|first1=Jan |last2=Santaeulalia|first2= Inés  |title=Gustavo Petro: 'Colombia doesn't need socialism, it needs democracy and peace' |url=https://english.elpais.com/usa/2021-09-22/gustavo-petro-colombia-doesnt-need-socialism-it-needs-democracy-and-peace.html |access-date=31 May 2022 |work=El País |date=22 September 2021 |location=Bogotá |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531164118/https://english.elpais.com/usa/2021-09-22/gustavo-petro-colombia-doesnt-need-socialism-it-needs-democracy-and-peace.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Of his campaign, [[Gwynne Dyer]] wrote: "Petro is a known quantity, active in politics for the past thirty years. He's not really radical, but he would be Colombia's first-ever president from the left, so for some Colombians his policies would seem extreme: things like expanding social programs, ending oil and gas exploration, and investing in agriculture."<ref name="Dyer 2022"/> His political party, [[Humane Colombia]], promoted the creation of the [[Historic Pact for Colombia]] coalition, which includes [[social movement]]s, [[socialist]], [[Natural environment|environmental]], and [[feminist]] associations.<ref name="auto"/> The ideological diversity of the coalition was seen as a source of internal tension, and Petro tried to win over more of the middle class during his campaign, which led him to moderate his economic program and his criticism of the private sector,<ref name="The Dialogue 2022"/> while trying to distance himself from Venezuela, which he previously supported; he maintained his position of re-establishing bilateral relations with the government of [[Nicolás Maduro]].<ref>{{Cite news|date=6 May 2022|title=Petro restablecería relaciones diplomáticas con la Venezuela de Nicolás Maduro|url=https://www.rcnradio.com/politica/petro-restableceria-relaciones-diplomaticas-con-la-venezuela-de-nicolas-maduro|access-date=7 May 2022|work=RCN Radio|language=es|archive-date=6 May 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220506185552/https://www.rcnradio.com/politica/petro-restableceria-relaciones-diplomaticas-con-la-venezuela-de-nicolas-maduro|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=6 May 2022 |title=Gustavo Petro prometió restablecer relaciones con Venezuela si llega a la Presidencia |url=https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2022/05/06/gustavo-petro-prometio-restablecer-relaciones-con-venezuela-si-llega-a-la-presidencia/ |access-date=2022-05-07 |work=Infobae |language=es-ES |archive-date=7 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220507214414/https://www.infobae.com/america/colombia/2022/05/06/gustavo-petro-prometio-restablecer-relaciones-con-venezuela-si-llega-a-la-presidencia/ |url-status=live }}</ref> During the campaign, he was critical of the [[neoliberal]] system of the [[Colombian economy]] and its reliance on oil and gas, advocated [[Progressivism|progressive]] proposals on [[women's rights]] and [[LGBTQ]] issues, and supported a peace agreement between the state and the guerrillas.<ref name=auto/><ref name="auto1">{{Cite news|url=https://www.pagina12.com.ar/392892-gustavo-petro-la-esperanza-progresista-para-superar-la-crisi|title=Gustavo Petro, la esperanza progresista para superar la crisis de Colombia &#124; En mayo los colombianos elegirán al sucesor del presidente Iván Duque|first=Guido|last=Vassallo|date=2 January 2022|work=Pagina 12|language=es|access-date=16 January 2022|archive-date=15 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220115145317/https://www.pagina12.com.ar/392892-gustavo-petro-la-esperanza-progresista-para-superar-la-crisi|url-status=live}}</ref> Proposals from Petro to change the nation's economic model by piling taxes on unproductive landowners, as well as abandoning oil and coal for clean energy, upset investors. Some feared his efforts to shift wealth from rich to poor could cause Colombia to become similar to present-day Venezuela. Critics claim his ideas are also similar to the early days of [[Hugo Chávez]]'s government in Venezuela.<ref>{{Cite news |date=24 May 2018 |title=Colombia's leftist Petro, the candidate who wants to upset the status quo |publisher=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-colombia-election-petro-idUSKCN1IP22P |access-date=13 May 2022 |last1=Murphy |first1=Helen |last2=Acosta |first2=Luis Jaime |archive-date=13 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220513085035/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-colombia-election-petro-idUSKCN1IP22P |url-status=live }}</ref> Petro was critical of the Maduro government's commitment to oil usage whilst on the campaign trail. In an interview with ''[[Le Monde]]'', Petro argued that "Maduro's Venezuela and Duque's Colombia are more similar than they seem", pointing to both governments' commitment to non-renewable energy and the "authoritarian drift" of the two. Regarding Chávez, Petro praised his efforts to bolster equality but said that Chávez "made a serious mistake of linking his social program to oil revenues".<ref>{{cite news |last=Delcas|first=Marie |title=Gustavo Petro: 'Maduro's Venezuela and Duque's Colombia are more similar than they seem' |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2022/05/26/gustavo-petro-maduro-s-venezuela-and-duque-s-colombia-are-more-similar-than-they-seem_5984776_4.html |access-date=31 May 2022 |work=Le Monde |date=26 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220527094552/https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2022/05/26/gustavo-petro-maduro-s-venezuela-and-duque-s-colombia-are-more-similar-than-they-seem_5984776_4.html |archive-date=27 May 2022|url-status=live|url-access=subscription|location=Bogotá}}</ref> During the campaign, Petro and his running mate [[Francia Márquez]] faced numerous death threats from [[Right-wing paramilitarism in Colombia|paramilitary groups]]. Petro cancelled rallies in the [[Colombian coffee region]] in early May 2022 after his security team uncovered an alleged plot by the La Cordillera gang.<ref>{{cite news |last=García|first=Geraldine |title=Colombia's Petro resumes election campaign after revealing attack plan |url=https://www.laprensalatina.com/colombias-petro-resumes-election-campaign-after-revealing-attack-plan/ |access-date=24 May 2022 |work=La Prensa Latina |date=5 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220506050544/https://www.laprensalatina.com/colombias-petro-resumes-election-campaign-after-revealing-attack-plan/ |archive-date=6 May 2022 |url-status=live|location=[[Cúcuta]]}}</ref><ref name=sirota>{{cite news |last=Sirota|first=Sara|title=International political actors condemn mounting violence in Colombia's presidential election |url=https://theintercept.com/2022/05/23/colombia-presidential-election-violence/ |access-date=24 May 2022 |work=The Intercept |date=24 May 2022 |archive-date=24 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220524005320/https://theintercept.com/2022/05/23/colombia-presidential-election-violence/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In response to this and other similar situations, 90 elected officials and prominent individuals from 20 countries signed an open letter expressing concern and condemnation of attempts of political violence against Márquez and Petro. The letter highlighted the assassination of over 50 social leaders, trade unionists, environmentalists, and other community representatives in 2022. Signatories of the letter included former Ecuadorian president [[Rafael Correa]], American linguist and philosopher [[Noam Chomsky]], and French member of the National Assembly [[Jean-Luc Mélenchon]].<ref name=sirota/> Petro received the support of [[Luis Gilberto Murillo]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Lo que dijo Luis Gilberto Murillo sobre por qué decidió unirse a la campaña de Gustavo Petro |url=https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/lo-que-dijo-luis-gilberto-murillo-sobre-por-que-decidio-unirse-a-la-campana-de-gustavo-petro.html |access-date=3 June 2022 |work=El País |date=31 May 2022 |language=es |archive-date=3 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220603085144/https://www.elpais.com.co/politica/lo-que-dijo-luis-gilberto-murillo-sobre-por-que-decidio-unirse-a-la-campana-de-gustavo-petro.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The [[conservative liberal]] coalition, [[Team for Colombia]], made up of significant figures ranging from the [[Centrism|centre]], [[centre-right]], and [[right-wing]], was placed as second most voted in some opinion polls. The coalition was seen as having strong support among the upper socio-economic strata in the big cities.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-01-02/rumbo-a-las-presidenciales-colombianas-muchos-candidatos-pero-tres-corrientes-de-voto.html|title=Rumbo a las presidenciales colombianas: muchos candidatos, pero tres corrientes de voto|first=Jorge|last=Galindo|date=2 January 2022|work=El País|language=es|access-date=16 January 2022|archive-date=15 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220115145313/https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-01-02/rumbo-a-las-presidenciales-colombianas-muchos-candidatos-pero-tres-corrientes-de-voto.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In August 2021, [[Federico Gutiérrez]], the former popular mayor of Medellín, completed the formal act to formalize his candidacy for the presidency independently, by collecting signatures without the support of any political party or having the backing of recognized politicians such as [[Álvaro Uribe]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pinto Duitama |first1=Karen |date=30 August 2021 |title=Federico Gutiérrez oficializa su candidatura a la presidencia con inscripción independiente |language=es |work=Asuntos Legales |url=https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/federico-gutierrez-oficializa-su-candidatura-a-la-presidencia-con-inscripcion-independiente-3224967 |access-date=14 April 2022 |archive-date=7 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220407144304/https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/federico-gutierrez-oficializa-su-candidatura-a-la-presidencia-con-inscripcion-independiente-3224967 |url-status=live }}</ref> As the [[Democratic Center (Colombia)|Democratic Center]] party nominee [[Óscar Iván Zuluaga]] withdrew his own candidacy and endorsed Gutiérrez, his opponents attempted to link his candidacy to controversial party members, including the founder and former president Uribe and incumbent president [[Iván Duque]], who suffered from high disapproval ratings. Gutiérrez also reportedly had lower name recognition than some of his opponents.<ref name="auto"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Palomino|first=Sally |title=Fico Gutiérrez y el riesgo de reconocerse como 'el candidato de Uribe' |url=https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-03-14/fico-gutierrez-y-el-riesgo-de-reconocerse-como-el-candidato-de-uribe.html |access-date=13 May 2022 |work=El País |date=14 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220512013633/https://elpais.com/internacional/2022-03-14/fico-gutierrez-y-el-riesgo-de-reconocerse-como-el-candidato-de-uribe.html |archive-date=12 May 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref> In November 2021, Gutiérrez joined other former public servers in his coalition, along with Enrique Peñalosa, Juan Carlos Echeverry, [[Dilian Francisca Toro]], David Barguil, and Alejandro Char. Gutiérrez took the second place in the polls at the end of October 2021.<ref>{{cite tweet |title=Polls Results October 2021 |accessdate=17 March 2022|user=AndresCamiloHR|number=1455510409528610821|author=Andrés Hernández Ramírez}}</ref> Due to his somewhat unexpected political success, he was invited to the debate of Prisa Media where he represented one of the three different political sectors of Colombia, debating with Petro and Fajardo. During the debate, Gutiérrez showed his opposition to Petro and gained favor among those who did not see Petro as the best option for Colombia.<ref>{{cite web |title=Polls Results March 2022 |date=11 March 2022 |url=https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/petro-fico-y-fajardo-ganarian-las-consultas-este-domingo-segun-ultimas-encuestas-3320419 |accessdate=17 March 2022 |archive-date=17 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220317120315/https://www.asuntoslegales.com.co/actualidad/petro-fico-y-fajardo-ganarian-las-consultas-este-domingo-segun-ultimas-encuestas-3320419 |url-status=live }}</ref> During the parliamentary elections on 13 March 2022, different consultations to elect a presidential candidate also took place. Gutiérrez and his coalition won first place with over 1.8 million votes, improving his chances of becoming President of Colombia in 2022.<ref>{{cite web |title=Victory of Equipo por Colombia during parliamentary elections |date=13 March 2022 |url=https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/federico-gutierrez-gana-consulta-de-equipo-por-colombia-va-por-la-presidencia-LO16903844 |accessdate=17 March 2022 |archive-date=14 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314123831/https://www.elcolombiano.com/colombia/politica/federico-gutierrez-gana-consulta-de-equipo-por-colombia-va-por-la-presidencia-LO16903844 |url-status=live }}</ref> Gutiérrez received backing from the [[Colombian Liberal Party|Liberal Party]], the [[Colombian Conservative Party|Conservative Party]], the [[Party of the U]], and the [[Radical Change]] party, which would have guaranteed him a majority in Congress if he was elected. Former president and Liberal Party chief [[César Gaviria]] explained his backing in a statement: "We are in total agreement that we must dedicate ourselves to the vulnerable, poor, marginalized people of this country, to the indigenous, the Afro-descendants, to young people." Gutiérrez also agreed to include anti-poverty and social development efforts in his manifesto, and a boost for education and health services.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Acosta |first=Luis Jaime |date=2022-04-27 |title=Colombia's Liberal Party backs center-right Gutierrez for president |publisher=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-liberal-party-backs-center-right-gutierrez-president-2022-04-27/ |access-date=2022-05-13 |archive-date=3 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220503224710/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-liberal-party-backs-center-right-gutierrez-president-2022-04-27/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In May 2022, ''[[El Espectador]]'' published an article exposing the connections of Gutiérrez's campaign chief, Cesar Giraldo, to the mafia and drug traffickers.<ref>{{cite news |title='Calzones', el hombre fuerte del uribismo que tuvo negocios con narcos y hace campaña por Fico |url=https://www.elespectador.com/investigacion/cesar-giraldo-calzones-el-uribista-de-pereira-que-hace-negocios-con-narcotraficantes-e-impulsa-la-campana-de-fico-gutierrez/ |newspaper=[[El Espectador]] |language=es |date=14 May 2022 |access-date=29 May 2022 |url-access=subscription |archive-date=29 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220529114129/https://www.elespectador.com/investigacion/cesar-giraldo-calzones-el-uribista-de-pereira-que-hace-negocios-con-narcotraficantes-e-impulsa-la-campana-de-fico-gutierrez/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Alsema|first=Adriaan|title=Establishment candidate in Colombia's elections linked to mafia |url=https://colombiareports.com/establishment-candidate-in-colombias-elections-linked-to-mafia/ |access-date=29 May 2022 |work=Colombia Reports |date=17 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220525175630/https://colombiareports.com/establishment-candidate-in-colombias-elections-linked-to-mafia/ |archive-date=25 May 2022 |url-status=live|language=es}}</ref>

The businessman and former mayor of Bucaramanga, [[Rodolfo Hernández Suárez]], backed by the [[far-right]] party [[League of Anti-Corruption Governors]], declared his candidacy in 2022 as an independent, with [[Marelen Castillo]] as his running-mate.<ref name="torrado1">{{cite web |last=Torrado |first=Santiago |date=2022-05-29 |title=Marelen Castillo, la desconocida vicepresidenta de Rodolfo Hernández |url=https://elpais.com/america-colombia/elecciones-presidenciales/2022-05-29/marelen-castillo-la-desconocida-vicepresidenta-de-rodolfo-hernandez.html |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=El País América Colombia |language=es |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530092624/https://elpais.com/america-colombia/elecciones-presidenciales/2022-05-29/marelen-castillo-la-desconocida-vicepresidenta-de-rodolfo-hernandez.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He campaigned against the corruption of the traditional political class and emphasizing his image as a successful entrepreneur who can transform Colombia.<ref name=":14">{{cite web |date=2022-05-29 |title=Así es Rodolfo Hernández, el 'Donald Trump' colombiano que quiere ser presidente |url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2022/05/29/rodolfo-hernandez-quien-es-perfil-trump-elecciones-candidato-presidencial-colombia-orix/ |access-date=2022-05-30 |website=CNN |language=es |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530170359/https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/2022/05/29/rodolfo-hernandez-quien-es-perfil-trump-elecciones-candidato-presidencial-colombia-orix/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He fully financed his own campaign, and promised to get rid of [[corruption in Colombia]].<ref name=":3"/> He proposed to declare a [[state of emergency]] for 90 days and suspend all judicial and administrative functions "in order to address corruption". Dyer commented: "He will rule by decree, in other words, and he gets to choose who is arrested. It could end up as a populist dictatorship."<ref name="Dyer 2022"/> He also promised major budget cuts, eliminating the use of presidential planes and helicopters, and donating all the money he would receive as president.<ref>{{cite web |last=Oquendo |first=Catalina |date=2022-05-30 |title=Rodolfo Hernández, the populist earthquake shaking up the Colombian election |url=https://english.elpais.com/international/2022-05-30/rodolfo-hernandez-the-populist-earthquake-shaking-up-the-colombian-election.html |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=EL PAÍS English Edition |language=en-us |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531073209/https://english.elpais.com/international/2022-05-30/rodolfo-hernandez-the-populist-earthquake-shaking-up-the-colombian-election.html |url-status=live }}</ref> He said he would give financial rewards to citizens who report corrupt state officials.<ref name="autogenerated1">{{Cite news |date=2022-05-30 |title=Populist Hernández to face leftwinger Petro in Colombia election run-off |work=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/8c7abbb0-4362-4d63-967a-55c436164c11 |access-date=2022-05-30 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530072225/https://www.ft.com/content/8c7abbb0-4362-4d63-967a-55c436164c11 |url-status=live }}</ref> He pledged to strengthen law and order and create jobs.<ref name=":3"/> He also praised [[Andrés Manuel López Obrador]] for his "anti-corruption efforts".<ref>{{cite web |title=Hernández dice que hará conferencias como López Obrador para exponer a quienes intenten sobornarlo |url=https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/video/rodolfo-hernandez-conferencias-prensa-lopez-obrador-candidato-elecciones-colombia-fernando-del-rincon-conclusiones-cnne/ |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=CNN |date=24 May 2022 |language=es |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531073210/https://cnnespanol.cnn.com/video/rodolfo-hernandez-conferencias-prensa-lopez-obrador-candidato-elecciones-colombia-fernando-del-rincon-conclusiones-cnne/ |url-status=live }}</ref> He was dubbed as the "king of TikTok" on several occasions because of his large following and his extensive campaign during the 2022 presidential elections on [[TikTok]].<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |last=Griffin |first=Oliver |date=2022-05-30 |title=Colombia's 'king of TikTok' Hernandez ready for run-off after shock result |publisher=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-king-tiktok-hernandez-ready-run-off-after-shock-result-2022-05-30/ |access-date=2022-05-30 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530172506/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-king-tiktok-hernandez-ready-run-off-after-shock-result-2022-05-30/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite news |date=2022-05-30 |title='TikTok King', 77, challenges ex-rebel for Colombia's top job |publisher=BBC |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61631964 |access-date=2022-05-30 |last=Buschschlüter|first= Vanessa |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530182416/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61631964 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=":5">{{cite news |last=Gandolfi |first=Sara |date=2022-05-30 |title=Colombia, slogan anti-corruzione e balletti sui social: il re di TikTok Hernandez va al ballottaggio |url=https://www.corriere.it/esteri/22_maggio_30/colombia-re-tiktok-hernandez-grande-sorpresa-presidenziali-e6f67ee4-dfff-11ec-907c-89e18a84369e.shtml |access-date=2022-05-30 |newspaper=Corriere della Sera |language=it |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530182415/https://www.corriere.it/esteri/22_maggio_30/colombia-re-tiktok-hernandez-grande-sorpresa-presidenziali-e6f67ee4-dfff-11ec-907c-89e18a84369e.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref> He did not claim to be on the right or the left;<ref name="torrado1"/> [[NACLA]] described his political position as a ''[[Realpolitik]]'' centrist,<ref>{{cite web |last1=López |first1=Ociel Alí |title=Colombia Elections: The End of Uribismo? |url=https://nacla.org/colombia-presidential-election-petro |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=NACLA |date=30 May 2022 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530210414/https://nacla.org/colombia-presidential-election-petro |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Reuters]] described him as centre-right,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Griffin |first=Oliver |date=2022-05-30 |title=Colombia's 'king of TikTok' Hernandez ready for run-off after shock result |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-king-tiktok-hernandez-ready-run-off-after-shock-result-2022-05-30/ |access-date=2022-05-31 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530172506/https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/colombias-king-tiktok-hernandez-ready-run-off-after-shock-result-2022-05-30/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and other analysts struggled to label him.<ref>{{cite web |date=2022-05-29 |title=Five things to know about Colombia's election |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61510332 |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=BBC News |last=Buschschlüter|first= Vanessa |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531222411/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61510332 |url-status=live }}</ref> Also described as a [[populist]], he was compared to [[Donald Trump]] and [[Silvio Berlusconi]].<ref name="autogenerated1"/> He supports the decriminalization of abortion under certain circumstances, as well as the legalization of marijuana for medical use.<ref>{{cite tweet|number=1528772296504225794|user=ingrodolfohdez|title=NO SE DEJEN ENGAÑAR🚨! Yo NO apoyo el Fracking ni el Glifosato. Y SI apoyo la legalización de la marihuana medicinal y el derecho al aborto bajo las condiciones estipuladas. No se confundan.|author=Rodolfo Hernández|date=23 May 2022}}</ref> He declared himself in favor of [[same-sex marriage]], [[Same-sex adoption|adoption]] of children by same-sex couples, and [[assisted suicide]].<ref>{{cite web |date=May 31, 2022 |title=Las 20 propuestas de Rodolfo Hernández que, según él, lo diferencian del uribismo |url=https://www.noticiascaracol.com/politica/elecciones-colombia/las-20-propuestas-de-rodolfo-hernandez-que-segun-el-lo-diferencian-del-uribismo-rg10 |url-status=live |archive-date=31 May 2022 |access-date=25 June 2022 |website=Noticias Caracol |language=es |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531195441/https://noticias.caracoltv.com/politica/elecciones-colombia/las-20-propuestas-de-rodolfo-hernandez-que-segun-el-lo-diferencian-del-uribismo-rg10?_amp=true }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=May 31, 2022 |title=¿Qué opinan candidatos presidenciales sobre decisión del suicidio médicamente asistido? |url=https://noticias.caracoltv.com/politica/elecciones-colombia/que-opinan-candidatos-presidenciales-sobre-decision-del-suicidio-medicamente-asistido-rg10 |url-status=live |archive-date=15 June 2022 |access-date= |website=Noticias Caracol |language=es-CO |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220615180831/https://noticias.caracoltv.com/politica/elecciones-colombia/que-opinan-candidatos-presidenciales-sobre-decision-del-suicidio-medicamente-asistido-rg10 }}</ref> His policies also included: lowering the [[value-added tax]] from 19% to 10%; a [[basic income]] for all senior citizens regardless of past contributions or lack thereof, and potentially those near or below the poverty line; progressively writing off debt for students in ''estrato'' 1 and 2 (both active students and those with the best grades); increased access to higher education in the regions; [[universal health care]]; switching from a punitive to a rehabilitative attitude towards drug addiction; granting [[Colombia at the Olympics|Olympians]] and world record holders from the country state pensions; increasing social payments for successful sportspeople to up to [[Colombian peso|COP]]100,000 per day; a 50% quota for women in public service and the presidential cabinet; welfare payments for those that maintain (rather than cut down) forested areas; and limiting [[fracking]] unless it meets environmental conditions.<ref name=":02">{{cite web |last=Pritchard |first=Oli |date=2022-05-21 |title=Election cheat sheets: Rodolfo Hernández |url=https://thebogotapost.com/election-cheat-sheets-rodolfo-hernandez/50005/ |access-date=2022-05-30 |website=The Bogotá Post |language=en-GB |archive-date=31 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220531020631/https://thebogotapost.com/election-cheat-sheets-rodolfo-hernandez/50005/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Regarding the [[Colombian peace process]], Hernández stated his willingness to add an addendum to the [[FARC peace deal]] to include the [[National Liberation Army (Colombia)|National Liberation Army]].<ref name=":14"/> He expressed support for a restoration of consular relations with Venezuela to address the violence on the border, saying: "Consular relations are necessary for good circulation, both commercial and touristic, and also because the border is where the increase in violence that Colombia is experiencing is also most felt."<ref>{{cite web |title=Rodolfo Hernández reprendrait les relations consulaires avec le Venezuela 'le même jour' |url=https://news.dayfr.com/nouvelles/amp/657013 |access-date=2022-05-31 |website=news.dayfr.com |archive-date=20 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220620074322/https://news.dayfr.com/nouvelles/amp/657013 |url-status=live }}</ref> He received the backing of the third-placed candidate Gutiérrez for the second round, who urged voters "to keep Petro out".<ref>{{cite news |date=2022-05-30 |title='TikTok King', 77, challenges ex-rebel for Colombia's top job|publisher=BBC |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61631964 |access-date=2022-05-30 |archive-date=30 May 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220530182416/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-61631964 |url-status=live |author=Buschschlüter, Vanessa}}</ref>

